## What to deliver

Build and rig the Runner up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches
the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly, and
the rig is complete: a low, long, wedge-shaped obsidian body on four thin, sprung legs,
a narrow head carrying a single acid-green eye slit, and a contiguous dorsal accent ridge
along the spine sculpted **only** in `#4a4358` and appearing nowhere else on the model.
**Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation
subcommands: a **`move`** (a light, darting run whose feet plant and whose body skitters
over them, authored in place) and an **`attack`** (a full-body lunge that coils, strikes,
and recovers while the feet stay planted). The animations and the accent region are the
fixed contract — produce all of them; the parts and joints that realize them are yours.
You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top, but do not drop or
contradict the required `move` and `attack`. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render`
so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part
is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim
render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an
animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs
and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
